  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1) \nWest to northwest flow aloft will lead to an active weather pattern \nthrough at least mid week and possibly lingering into the later part\nof the week. \n\nThe airmass across our area has destabilized nicely again this \nafternoon, with an axis of MLCapes of 2000 J/KG or so running \nnorthwest to southeast across our area. This is resulting is a tight\ninstability gradient stretching from far northwest Indiana down \nacross northern Ohio. There is some uncertainty as to how well the \nstorms currently up toward southern Lake Michigan will hold \ntogether, but if they do, they could track east southeast along the \ngradient and toward our area late this afternoon into early evening. \nWill go ahead and include some lower end pops across our far north \nthis evening to account for this possibility. \n\nA strengthening low level jet later tonight will likely lead to some\nadditional redevelopment off to our northwest. Several of the hi- \nres models are suggesting this activity will then drop east- \nsoutheast into our area late tonight into early Monday morning. With \nthis being during a less favorable time of day, would generally \nexpect a weakening trend with any storms that do make it into our \narea, but suppose it will be tough to rule out a low end \nsevere/damaging wind threat, especially across northwest portions of \nour area. \n\nRemnants from the potential morning activity could initially affect \ntemperatures. Have made some minor downward adjustments to \ntemperatures as a result, but we will still be quite humid with \nafternoon heat indices in the 95 to 100 degree range. This would \nstill allow for a fair amount of destabilization through the day so \nadditional showers and thunderstorms will be possible later Monday \nafternoon and into the overnight hours as a weak frontal boundary \npushes south toward our area. With strengthening low/mid level flow,\nsome severe storms will again be possible. Damaging wind will be the\nprimary threat, although increasing low level shear could also lead \nto a lower end tornado threat, especially for areas along and north \nof the I-70 corridor. \n\nIn the persistent northwest flow aloft, additional rounds of showers\nand storms will be possible Tuesday and Wednesday as the frontal \nboundary continues to sag slowly south into our area. A chance for \nprecipitation will linger into Thursday and possibly Friday, with \nthe best chances across our southern areas, closer to the remnant \nboundary.\n\nWith high summertime PWs in place and repeated rounds of storms \npossibly tracking over the same locations, flooding concerns will \nincrease as we progress into mid week.",
